--TEST--
`Core\Sse::upgrade` takes `spawn script`'s operand and refuses a request no server is answering, rather than answering as though a stream had opened — ADR 0083 § 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0083 § 5 makes an event stream the same root isolate § 1 opens, prepared
// by this member and started by the connection that is still sending the
// response it writes into. The cell that hand-over is left in is offered to
// every request a server runs -- and a script run from the command line is not
// a request a server is answering, so there is none here. The alternative is
// the one this case exists to refuse: answering `void` and leaving a program
// to believe its client was going to receive events.
try {
    Core\Sse::upgrade("streams/feed.nvs");
    echo "opened\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The second parameter is optional, and omitting it above is not what was
// refused -- an entry declaring no parameter needs no `args:`, exactly as
// `spawn script` with no `with(args:)` clause does. So the same report arrives
// either way, which is what says the refusal is about the missing request and
// not about the call's shape.
try {
    Core\Sse::upgrade("streams/feed.nvs", args: {room: "lobby"});
    echo "opened\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "refused again\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Sse::upgrade` needs a request a server is answering and this is not one: every request the server runs is offered an event stream, and a program run from the command line, a `spawn script` child and a `#[Test]` method are not requests
refused again
